      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Trust-on-first-use CA certificate is distributed with no out-of-band integrity check, enabling MITM during pairing.\n\nttDrop's whole HTTPS trust model rests on a device permanently installing the CA certificate served here at GET /ca.crt (registered unauthenticated in TtDropServer.java:80). The endpoint, cert-help.html, the QR code, and app.js never present any fingerprint/hash of the CA for the user to verify out-of-band before trusting it — the client simply downloads and trusts whatever bytes arrive over the network at first-pairing time.\n\nImpact: An attacker positioned on the LAN during a victim's first connection (ARP spoofing, rogue Wi-Fi AP, compromised switch) can intercept the initial handshake — which necessarily presents an as-yet-untrusted, self-signed leaf certificate the browser will let the user click through — and/or intercept the plain download of /ca.crt, substituting their own CA certificate. The victim, following the app's own instructions, installs the attacker's CA as a trusted root. From then on the attacker can transparently and persistently MITM all of that device's ttDrop traffic (and, because installed CAs are usually trusted system/browser-wide, potentially other HTTPS traffic on that device too) with no further indication to the user.\n\nExploit scenario: Victim starts ttDrop in HTTPS mode on a shared/open Wi-Fi network and scans the printed QR code / navigates to the shown URL to pair. An attacker on the same network path answers ARP requests or runs a rogue AP, intercepts the TLS handshake (whose leaf certificate is not yet trusted by anything, so a substituted self-signed cert looks the same to the browser) and the follow-on GET /ca.crt, and returns their own CA certificate and leaf certificate. The victim installs the attacker's CA per cert-help.html's instructions, after which the attacker can decrypt/modify all files transferred and inject files as if they were the real ttDrop host.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker has an active on-path position on the LAN during the victim's very first pairing/CA-install (ARP spoofing, rogue AP, etc.).; Victim completes the documented cert-install workflow (clicks through any browser warning and installs the fetched CA certificate).\n\nRecommendation: Give the user an out-of-band way to verify the CA before trusting it — e.g. display the CA certificate's SHA-256 fingerprint in the desktop GUI (a channel the network attacker does not control) and have cert-help.html/app.js show the same fingerprint for the user to compare before installing, or pin/verify the fingerprint embedded in the QR-code payload itself.\n\nCWE-322. Severity HIGH, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/main/java/ttdrop/server/CaCertHandler.java",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
